# What `jointAngle` is on HingeJoint2D

**`jointAngle` = connected-body rotation minus own rotation.** The joint
lives on the child segment, `connectedBody` is the parent, so at the knee this is
“thigh angle minus shin angle”, not the other way around.

Verified on a deep squat, where the lumbar is jammed exactly at its limit
`−20.00`, and `pelvis − torso` in the same frame gives `−19.74`. The reverse would
have given `+19.74` — a 40° miss.

You **cannot** check this sign by summing the chain angles “pelvis → hip → knee → ankle”:
with a flat foot the sum is near zero, and zero is symmetric,
so both variants pass the check the same way. You need a joint that sits far from
zero, and independently measured orientations of both bodies.

Hence the geometry: a positive joint angle rotates the child segment
**clockwise** relative to the parent, that is, takes its lower end backward
(into −X when looking right).

The human faces right: the toe is at +0.195 from the ankle, the heel at −0.065.
So for human flexion
- the **knee** must grow positive (heel back) — limit `0…+120`;
- the **elbow** must grow negative (hand forward and up) — limit `−140…0`.

Knee and elbow bend in opposite directions, so the same sign
of the range on both is always an error. That is exactly how it was found that the knee sat
mirrored: `−120…0` allowed only ostrich flexion, the knee went
4 cm past the hip–ankle line in stance and 12 cm in a squat.

# Single sign convention

A positive controller signal means “increase `jointAngle`”, and that is
extensor work. Verified on the knee: a counterclockwise torque (flexor,
`direction = +1`) decreases `jointAngle`. This matches the formula above:
a counterclockwise torque increases own rotation, and that enters
`jointAngle` with a minus sign.

```csharp
// ✅ this is how ApplySignal is built — use it, do not write your own
if (signal > 0f) { UpdateMuscle(extensor, signal); UpdateMuscle(flexor, 0f); }
else             { UpdateMuscle(extensor, 0f); UpdateMuscle(flexor, -signal); }
```

A flipped sign does not give an obvious error: it turns the controller into
positive feedback, and the joint drives into the stop. That is how the neck dropped
the head to ±30° in two seconds.

# The ankle is a special joint

The foot is pressed to the ground, so muscle torque does not go into joint rotation,
but into turning the whole body. Hence two bans:

- Do not add a PD to a target angle on the ankle. The spring drops the human in
  2.5 s at either sign.
- Do not damp the ankle angle with a muscle. `ankleD 1.0` drops in 2.8 s.
  Viscosity is supplied by passive `JointFriction`.

The ankle gets only a torque against center-of-mass offset.

The foot is one rectangle. A second segment (toes) does not pay off
on a hundred creatures: a free metatarsal hinge breaks the ankle, and a weld gives
two extra `Rigidbody2D` with no new degree of freedom.

# The lumbar holds the world vertical, not the angle to the pelvis

The torso is split into `Pelvis` and `Torso` with a lumbar hinge. A positional PD to
the angle relative to the pelvis does not work there: the torso does not know where up is, and when
the pelvis topples it obediently rides down with it, adding 35 kg to the fall.

The error is taken from `VestibularSystem.GetBodyTilt()` (absolute torso tilt),
the derivative — from `GetBodyAngularVelocity()`. Mixing the world error with
`joint.jointSpeed` is forbidden; those are different reference frames. The field and CLI
`lumbarPGain` / `-lumbarP` are **plus 2**. The minuses are already in the formula
`(-P)*error - (-D)*speed`; you must not pass a negative value, that
will invert the loop. Switching from the angle to the pelvis to the world vertical gave torso
tilt 3.02° versus 8.43°, at the same RMS.

The ban on positional PD applies only to the ankle: it is special because
the foot is pressed to the ground. The lumbar connects two free segments; a spring
to a target orientation is legal there.

Watch the joint’s operating point, not only the maximum. In the current
baseline 10.9° is the peak of the startup transient, not a constant operating point;
after a 22 N·s push the lumbar reaches 13.5° of the ±20° range. With fast
muscles it slams into the stop. Pelvis tilt must be logged — with the torso split
this is a separate equilibrium variable.

# A light joint needs Stable PD, not coefficient tuning

Ordinary PD is unstable when `K·Δt/I` is large: in a step it applies a torque
computed from the velocity at the **start** of the step, and overshoots the target. At the neck
`K·Δt/Ieff` ≈ 115, at the head ≈ 119 — there this is not margin, it is a guarantee of oscillation.
That is how the head thrashed: 400 reversals in 6 seconds, 33 Hz, up to 0.9° per step.

Coefficient tuning does not fix this, and both attempts are recorded as dead ends.
Lowering `neckMuscleTorque` from 15 to 4 N·m removes chatter by a factor of 35, but drops
the forward push from 21 to 18 N·s. Lowering `neckD` from 0.4 to 0.2 removes chatter
by a factor of 22, but the neck punches through its ±30° stop and 5.7 kg slam the torso. The same
term holds against a push and creates chatter: with ordinary PD this is inseparable.

It is fixed by a formula — Stable PD (Tan, Liu, Turk, 2011). Torque is computed from
the **predicted** angle at the end of the step, and the result is divided by `1 + K·Δt/I`:

```csharp
// ✅ this is how it stands in ControlSegmentToWorldUpright
float tiltForError = tilt + angVel * dt;                  // angle at the end of the step
float damping      = k * dt / effectiveInertia;           // k in N·m·s/rad
float signal       = Mathf.Clamp(raw / (1f + damping), -1f, 1f);
```

The denominator is not a fudge: it equals the exact solution of the implicit step for
a linear damper, so it is stable at any `K`. Take **paired** inertia,
`1/(1/Iself + 1/Iconn)`, for the same reasons as in `JointFriction`.

`Rigidbody2D.inertia` in `Awake` has not yet been recomputed from the collider, so
compute paired inertia lazily, on the first `FixedUpdate`.

The change is purely formulaic: at the same 15 N·m torque and the same `neckD` 0.4
chatter fell by a factor of 17 per step, torso tilt 2.16° → 1.43°, and both push
thresholds rose — forward 22 → 23, backward 21 → 23. This is the only case in
the project where rest and stability improved at the same time.

# Passive friction: an explicit damper must be limited

`JointFriction` computes `τ = −K·ω` and applies a pair to both bodies, so
angular acceleration equals `τ/I`, where `I` is the body’s inertia about **its own
center of mass** (`Rigidbody2D.inertia`), not about the joint. Explicit
integration is stable only at `K·Δt/I < 2`; above that the damper overshoots
through zero velocity and accelerates the joint, and a torque ceiling only turns
divergence into a limit cycle.

So the torque is cut twice: first to the magnitude that exactly stops
relative rotation in one step, then by `maxTorque`.

```
τ_max = ω_rel / ((1/I₁ + 1/I₂) · Δt)
```

With this limit the damper is stable at any `K`, but too large a `K`
turns the joint into an almost welded seam: it kills rotation in one step.
When picking `K`, look at the time constant `τ = I/K` — it does not depend on
the physics step, unlike the stability condition itself.

# Torque ceilings are derived from geometry

The foot will not transmit more torque than `m*g*(moment arm to support edge)`: beyond that
the body simply rotates about the toe or the heel. At 70 kg, toe 0.195 and
heel 0.065 that is 65 N·m on the extensor and 22 N·m on the flexor of each foot.
The 3:1 ratio matches reality: the calf is three times stronger than the anterior
tibialis.
